---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt, section } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import appSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam?raw";

const payload = excerpt(
  appSource,
  "pub type BoardPresence {",
  "fn encode_presence(",
  "fn presence_decoder(",
);
const presenceEffect = excerpt(
  appSource,
  "fn presence_effect(",
  "fn current_presence(",
);
const announce = excerpt(appSource, "fn announce_focus(");
const focusClicked = section(
  appSource,
  "    FocusClicked(id) -> {",
  "    PresenceStarted(handle) -> {",
);
const presenceEvents = section(
  appSource,
  "    PresenceEvent(event) ->",
  "    ReconnectClicked ->",
);
const remotePeers = excerpt(appSource, "fn remote_peers(");
const focusNames = excerpt(appSource, "fn focus_names(");
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="presence"
  description="Step five of the watershed build guide: publish the note a teammate is reading through the presence tier, where it expires with the connection, instead of storing it in the document where it would be sequenced, summarised, and replayed forever."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Some facts should be allowed to expire</h2>
    <p>
      Notes and votes both had to survive: that was the whole argument of the
      last two sheets. “Ada is reading this card right now” is the opposite kind
      of fact. Put it in the document and it gets sequenced, summarised, and
      replayed to whoever opens the board next Tuesday, long after it stopped
      being true.
    </p>
    <p>
      watershed’s answer is a second tier that runs alongside your channels.
      <strong>Ripples</strong> are the raw form: document-scoped broadcasts to
      every other connected client, with no order, no storage, and no
      convergence, on purpose. <strong>Presence</strong> is the roster built on
      top, and it is the one you want here — because a raw ripple leaves a peer’s
      last marker stuck on screen after they close the tab.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Declare what a teammate broadcasts</h2>
    <p>
      A presence payload is a record you own, with an encoder and a decoder.
      The tutorial’s is three fields: a colour, a short name, and the note this
      person is looking at — <code>None</code> when they are looking at the board
      as a whole.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={payload} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Note the <code>optional_field</code> on the way in. A peer running last
      week’s build might not send <code>focused_note</code> at all, and the
      roster should shrug at that rather than drop the peer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>One effect owns the heartbeat</h2>
    <p>
      <code>watershed_lustre.presence</code> is a single effect that starts the
      driver, hands back a <code>Handle</code> to keep in the model, and
      dispatches roster events as they arrive. You do not write a heartbeat, a
      TTL, or a reaper.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={presenceEffect} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Two implementations, one API.</strong> Where the server offers
        connection-backed presence, it tracks each socket directly: a late
        joiner gets the whole roster at once and a dropped connection removes
        its entry immediately. Where it doesn’t, presence falls back to
        heartbeating over ripples and expiring peers on a TTL. The
        <a href="/runtime/presence">runtime note</a> covers which you get and
        what changes when it’s the fallback.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Publishing a change is republishing the whole payload</h2>
    <p>
      Presence is state, not a stream of edits. Focusing a note updates the local
      model, then re-announces the current payload in full — so there is exactly
      one function that says what this client looks like to everyone else, and no
      way for the announced state and the real state to drift.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={announce} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={focusClicked} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Clicking the focused note again clears the focus, which is why the label
      flips between <code>Focus</code> and <code>Focused</code>. The button is a
      toggle over one <code>Option</code>, not two separate messages.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The roster arrives as events, including the bad ones</h2>
    <p>
      <code>State</code> is the full roster; <code>Changed</code> carries the
      delta and the full roster, and the tutorial re-renders from the roster in
      both cases rather than trying to patch. Rendering the whole list is cheap
      and it cannot get out of sync.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={presenceEvents} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The failure arms are the interesting part. A single peer whose payload
      doesn’t decode is ignored — one teammate on a bad build should not empty
      the roster. A server without presence support, or one that rejects the
      join, is a message on screen. Neither of them stops the board: notes and
      votes keep working, because presence was never load-bearing.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={remotePeers} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Your own entry is in that roster too. Filtering it out by session means the
      chip row can render “you” once, deliberately, instead of showing you twice
      and calling it a bug report.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>Deepening: presence is a read model over both tiers</h3>
    <p>
      The highlight on a card is where the two tiers meet. The note id comes from
      the durable channel; the “who is looking at it” comes from the ephemeral
      one; the card is a join over both, computed at render time.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={focusNames} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      A peer can be focused on a note that hasn’t reached your replica yet. The
      board says “a note that has not synced yet” rather than inventing a card,
      and corrects itself when the note lands. If you take one habit from this
      sheet, take that one: let the ephemeral tier point at durable state, never
      duplicate it.
    </p>
    <p>
      High-frequency signals — cursor motion, a typing indicator — stay as raw
      ripples. Presence is for membership and the slower status attached to it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Click <strong>Focus</strong> in one tab and watch the card light up in the
      other, with the roster naming who is on it — then close the tab and watch
      the marker leave on its own.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
